The file still displays mojibake in the console despite setting OutputEncoding. This is because the PowerShell console encoding setting may not take effect for this particular display, or the file has some encoding issue. But actually looking closely, the characters appear as mojibake because the terminal is interpreting UTF-8 bytes as GBK. Wait, actually the mojibake pattern "閲囬泦" = UTF-8 bytes of "采集" interpreted as GBK. So the file is UTF-8, but the console output is showing GBK interpretation of those UTF-8 bytes.

Hmm, but I set `[Console]::OutputEncoding = UTF8`. The issue might be that `Get-Content` is decoding the file using the wrong encoding, then re-encoding. Actually `Get-Content -Raw` reads with the default encoding (which on Windows PowerShell 5.1 is... ANSI/GBK by default). So Get-Content reads UTF-8 bytes as GBK → produces mojibake strings → then output re-encoded. So the fix is to use `Get-Content -Encoding UTF8`.

Let me re-read using `Get-Content -Encoding UTF8`. This matters for accurate verification of the report text, especially the "26 vs 6" discrepancy.
